<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/451">451</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Châlons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Catalaunian Plains was fought in 451 in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the Hunnic army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> and a coalition led by the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> and the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a>. Theodoric was killed, but Attila withdrew from Gaul. It was one of the last great campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-catalaunian-plains.jpg" alt="Battle of the Catalaunian Plains" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johannes de Thurocz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thur%C3%B3czy_kr%C3%B3nika_-_Attila_kir%C3%A1ly_csat%C3%A1ja_a_catalaunumi_s%C3%ADkon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By 450 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had restored their authority in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, though control of the provinces beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was diminishing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> occupying Roman territory were bound by treaty as foederati under their own leaders: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> were restive, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> awaited an opening for revolt, and the Alans on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> were more loyal. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> invaded, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> moved his army rapidly from Italy to Gaul and tried to persuade the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a> to join him. Theodoric hesitated, and Aetius turned to the former Praetorian Prefect of Gaul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, who according to tradition persuaded the Visigoths and other wavering peoples.</p><p>The coalition assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, reaching it on 14 June. Jordanes lists among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a>'s allies <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, Alans and other Celtic or German tribes; Peter Heather suggests the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> may have fielded about 25,000 men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> was leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with most of his objectives achieved when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Aetius</a> and his allies pursued him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>. The armies met on the Catalaunian Plains on or about 20 June. The exact site is uncertain; scholars place it near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalons-en-champagne">Châlons-en-Champagne</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, and the ridge at Montgueux, west of Troyes, is the most thoroughly researched proposal.</p><p>The account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>, who wrote long afterwards and is described as notoriously unreliable on Gothic history, tells of a struggle for the ridge, with the Alans in the centre. Theodoric was killed on the right, and his son Thorismund attacked the Hunnic centre and nearly killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>. Darkness fell in confusion, and neither side knew the outcome until morning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hydatius">Hydatius</a> says almost 300,000 men are said to have fallen. Attila withdrew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassiodorus">Cassiodorus</a> in the early sixth century the battle was increasingly seen as a Roman allied victory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> called it the last victory achieved in the name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, and Edward Shepherd Creasy called it a triumph of Christian Europe. Most modern authors have moved away from this view. J. B. Bury held that its significance had been enormously exaggerated, since the decisive moment was the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and the battle was fought as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> retreated, though it damaged his prestige and weakened his forces.</p><p>In 452 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> after a three-month siege; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-i">Pope Leo I</a> took part in an embassy that brought a treaty, and Attila retreated, most likely because of famine and disease in his army. Attila died in 453, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454 the coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> and their Germanic vassals gradually disintegrated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Catalaunian Plains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Others served Rome: Alans under Sangiban fought on the Roman side against Attila at the <b>Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</b> in 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Frankish federates fought the Vandals as they approached the Rhine in 406, and Frankish troops served under Aetius against Attila at the <b>Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</b> in 451, while other Franks backed the Huns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 451 Theodoric fought beside Flavius Aetius against Attila at the <b>Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</b> and was killed as the Huns were driven back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Battle of the Catalaunian Plains</b> in 451, where Aetius and the Visigoths halted the Hunnic Empire under Attila, is named after their land.</blockquote><a href="/state/catalauni">Catalauni</a></li></ul></section>
